From 0644e0bc94113b31573e8b3cc35aab1fd8ca0ee4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 04:32:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Hades][dvbridge] A picture drawn again with something over
 it takes what its frame before made

A frame with the GUI or subtitles over it that shows the same picture as the frame before it (a pause
with the OSD over it, a picture shown for another refresh) was composed and converted again in full at
every refresh: libplacebo's composition, the rows of 4:2:2 chroma and the codes of the IPT tunnel in
the source transport, and the whole libplacebo render (the mapping to the display in Low-Latency) in
the converted one. The same inputs give the same bytes, so nothing of that is made again now.

render() keeps a key of what it draws from: the GL textures of the planes of the picture and of its
layer with their offsets and depth, the times of both, the bytes of the metadata, the place and the
part of the picture drawn, the active area of the prepared metadata, the target, and every setting of
the renderer that the composition, the rows, the IPT codes or the converted picture depend on (the
step-down level, the composer, the transfer of the base layer, the transports asked for, Low-Latency
with its mapping, test pattern, HDR10+ tone curve, trims, 3D LUT and gamut table, and what failed on
this GPU). A render of a frame with something drawn over it (dvbridge_render_set_drawn()) whose key
and metadata are those of the render before takes:
 - in the source transport, the composed planes, their rows and the IPT codes as that render left
   them (the GUI is composited into rgb, which pass 3 makes again from them, and the frame is packed
   as before);
 - converted, rgb as that render left it, where nothing was drawn into it since (the GUI composited by
   the packer); else a copy of it that the first render of the same picture again keeps (132 MB at
   3840x2160, made once and freed when the stream ends). The caller says when it draws into rgb
   (dvbridge_render_texture_drawn(), as CDVBridgeGLES::PQTextureDrawn() always did).
Any other render, a failure, a recover or reset, the HDR10, plain and graphics renders, the warm-up
and a change of the tunnel forget it. A frame with nothing over it is not affected: the window system
keeps such a picture on the screen, and where it does not, it is drawn whole as before.

Such a frame costs less than a picture: the window system does not take its GPU time for the levels
of the frames with GUI elements (CDVGuiLevel, CDVGuiStep) nor for presenting a frame ahead
(CDVBridgeGLES::Reused()), as it does not for a picture that stands still. kodi.log says once in a
video that it happened. <video><dvreuse>false</dvreuse></video> in advancedsettings.xml turns it off,
to measure what it saves.

Measured on the software renderer (zink over lavapipe, LP_NUM_THREADS=4; the CPU does the GPU's
work, so this is a proxy), pictures 51 and 960 of a profile 7 FEL stream with the bench's GUI of
bands over 1200 of the 2160 rows (opaque, half and nearly transparent), a frame drawn again (render,
composite and pack; the render alone in parentheses):
 - YCbCr as composed: 266 ms (193) before, 100 ms (27) after;
 - IPT tunnel: 395 ms (250) before, 186 ms (41) after;
 - converted: 213 ms (175) before, 46 ms (7) after;
 - Low-Latency mapped to 700 cd/m2: 299 ms (264) before, 43 ms (7) after.
Every file the same bytes with it and without it (tools/regress/dvbridge, stage reuse, with four
mutants caught by the bytes).
